    "created_at": "2026-11-23T06:49:05Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-11-23T06:49:05Z",
    "body": "fix on issue/2970-rotary-parallel-band · PR #3650 · pinned by the_rotary_parallel_goes_all_the_way_round_a_laid_bar, the_rotary_parallel_goes_all_the_way_round_at_a_fine_stepover, a_bottom_over_the_axis_keeps_the_cut_to_the_upper_arc, a_full_turn_rotary_parallel_refuses_on_a_head_that_cannot_reach_under · merges when reviewed\n\nThank you for the report and the pictures. You were correct: the bottom of the bar stayed uncut.\n\nThe cause: Rotary Parallel planned the bar one time, from above only. The bottom quarter of the turn was not cut, and no note told you. On a test bar 126 degrees stayed uncut. On the example journal about 90 degrees stayed uncut.\n\nThe fix: Rotary Parallel now plans the bar in two halves, the top and the bottom, and joins them. With Bottom Z at the underside of the bar (the example: -0.375 in), the passes go all the way round. A Bottom Z above the axis still keeps the cut to the upper arc. The operation now shows a note when the passes go all the way round, because the machine must turn the part through a full turn of A.\n\nPlease try your part on the branch `issue/2970-rotary-parallel-band` and tell us if a band stays uncut.\n",
    "author_association": "OWNER",
    "pin": null,
